WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.340
Last video I showed you China's toughest exam,
the gaokao, and today, I'd like to take a

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:14.460
look at how it compares to another notoriously
difficult exam, India's JEE Advanced. I'll

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.140
show you some of the toughest problems from
each exam. But first, let's think about how

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:25.590
they compare overall. Both exams are taken
to gain entrance into university. China's

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:32.050
gaokao is sat by around 10 million students
each year. And to be eligible a student needs

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:39.040
to have finished high school, India's JEE
Advanced is sat by only around 200,000 students.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.360
But to get there you need to have already
passed through one round of exams, the JEE

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.510
Mains and made the cutoff so only the best
of the best students are able to sit the exam.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:57.830
The JEE is an engineering exam and assesses
physics, chemistry and math, allowing entry

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:04.280
into the Indian Institutes of Technology,
such as IIT Bombay with a world ranking of

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:14.210
172 and IIT Delhi, with a world ranking of
193. The gaokao has a broader scope, covering

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:20.700
the science subjects as electives, as well
as languages and essays. Doing well could

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.250
allow entry into some of the world's best
universities, including Tsinghua with a world

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:31.550
ranking of 15. And Peking at 23.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:37.650
Preparation for both exams is intense, and
starts years before the exam, with both countries

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:45.120
having a lot of private exam coaching. And
China has Maotanchang, a town almost entirely

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.980
dedicated to helping students score highly.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:54.900
I would say the stakes seem a little higher
in China, just because if you fail the exam,

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:01.050
your only options are to either give up or
wait an entire year to try again. Whereas

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:08.569
in India, there are a few other pathways and
other exams, such as UPSC or NEET.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:15.260
So let's see how the actual questions compare.
In my last gaokao video, I showed a math question

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.590
that many of you said was a bit on the easy
side. So today, I've picked a harder one.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:25.250
This is from the 2016 Jiangsu exam.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:33.069
As shown below, there is a straight line l
given by this equation, and a parabola C given

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:41.459
by this equation. Part one asks, If line l
passes the focus of the parabola, then what

00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:49.150
is the equation of the parabola? So they're
asking you really to find out what p is. And

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:54.209
that determines the shape, somewhere in here
would be the focus of the parabola. And you

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:59.150
can see in the solutions that I've written
out that this first part would be handy to

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.690
know how to find the focus of the parabola.
And if you did, you'd be able to find the

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:10.459
focus for our one to be p over two, zero,
then put that point into the line since we

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:16.690
know the line passes it and you will find
that p is equal to four. So you get your parabola

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.560
equation.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:25.959
Part two is more difficult. It says there
are two points P and Q on our parabola, such

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:34.319
that P and Q are symmetric with respect to
line l. a) prove that the midpoint of the

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:38.030
line segment PQ is this.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:44.250
Pause the video here and try it yourself to
really see how tricky this question can be.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:50.209
I interpret points P and Q to look something
like this. And a bit of information we know

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:58.770
is that a line between P and Q must be perpendicular
to l. Since the points are symmetric. From

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:05.010
the equation of the line l we can work out
its gradient is one. So the gradient of line

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:12.250
PQ must be equal to minus one. That's the
first brainwave that you need to get going.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:18.519
You can write the gradient of PQ from the
equation you know about the parabola and simplify

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:25.659
it down into this form where y one plus y
two over two is equal to minus p. Now that

00:04:25.659 --> 00:04:29.069
in fact will be the y component of the midpoint.

00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:34.979
Now we just need to find the x component.
To do that you'll need the second brainwave,

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:41.300
which is to remember that the midpoint of
PQ must lie on the line l since P and Q are

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:47.039
an equal distance from l. Use the equation
of line l, put in the coordinates of the midpoint

00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:54.099
and then you will find the x component of
the midpoint to be two minus p. Giving the

00:04:54.099 --> 00:04:56.729
final answer to that part of the question.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:02.520
Part B I stared at for a long time before
I was able to figure out what you meant to

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:09.710
do, they're asking what is the range of possible
values of p, p appears in this midpoint of

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:16.240
PQ and so there must only be a range of values
for p for which this is possible. The way

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.449
that I ended up doing this was to write an
equation of the line PQ, which was something

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:27.749
we hadn't done yet. Although we do already
know the gradient, and we know it passes through

00:05:27.749 --> 00:05:34.479
the midpoint, so we're able to write this
equation as y is minus x plus two minus two

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:40.550
p, then combine this linear equation with
the equation of the parabola that we were

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:48.240
given y squared is to p x, that will ultimately
give you this quadratic equation here, which

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.889
is a relationship between y and the parameter
p.

00:05:52.889 --> 00:06:00.009
To find the range of values of p, we're going
to be using the discriminant. The discriminant

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:08.159
of a quadratic in this form a x squared plus
bx plus c would be b squared minus four ac.

00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:14.610
And if that discriminant is bigger than zero,
they will be two real and unequal routes or

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:21.750
solutions. And that is the situation that
we want. So we work out our discriminant,

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:29.279
set it as being larger than zero, and find
that p would have to be between zero and four

00:06:29.279 --> 00:06:30.520
thirds.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:36.089
I found that was really challenging, not so
much that any of the individual steps were

00:06:36.089 --> 00:06:41.470
too hard to do. But it took a lot of forethought
to think of a method you would use to get

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.029
to the answer.

00:06:43.029 --> 00:06:48.979
A tough JEE advanced question that I found
on a similar topic is this one here from the

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:51.460
2018 paper 2.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:07:00.869
Let T be the line passing through the points
P and Q, let F1 be the set of all pairs of

00:07:00.869 --> 00:07:11.740
circles, S1 and S2, such that T is tangent
to S1 at P and tangent to S2 at Q and also

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:20.550
such that S1 and S2 touch each other at a
point say M. Let E1 be the set representing

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:31.319
the locus of M as the pair S1, S2 varies in
F1. Let the set of all straight line segments

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:40.479
joining a pair of distinct points of E1 and
passing through the point (1,1) be F2. let

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:49.729
E2 be the set of the midpoints of the line
segments in the set F2, then, which of the

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:52.900
following statements are true?

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:59.419
Now this question is completely out of control,
and seems to get worse with every single sentence.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:02.320
Again, pause here to have a little try yourself.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:09.429
It sounds impossible to understand when you
read it. But I think something like this is

00:08:09.429 --> 00:08:17.080
going on. We have our points P and Q, and
circles which are tangent at those points.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:22.499
These circles can change in size, but they
will always touch each other at this point

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:23.499
M.

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:29.719
Here, it's essential to know what a locus
is. And it is the set of all points whose

00:08:29.719 --> 00:08:37.050
locations satisfies a given condition. So
we're asked to find E1, which is the locus

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:44.870
of M. And that's sort of all the places that
M could possibly be, you first need to notice

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:52.840
that the angle between P M and Q will be 90
degrees. And that gives you the condition

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:59.460
you need to work out all the places M could
be because with that condition, we know that

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:07.980
the gradient of PM times the gradient of QM
would be equal to minus one. These lines are

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:15.760
perpendicular. Plug in your points and your
workout this equation, which is E1, all the

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.120
possible spots where M could be.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:26.120
Now E1 itself is actually a circle. And around
about here we could eliminate A as an answer.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:34.550
A is actually claiming that point P lies in
E1. But if M was over here, where P is then

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:42.620
the size of this circle would be radius zero,
and this one would have to be a straight line.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:49.220
So we can say that both P and Q are not actually
possible locations for M

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:55.950
and therefore not included in E1. So the circle
I guess is open at those points.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:10:03.770
The next thing that happens is that we start
to draw chords in this circle of E1, and these

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:11.010
chords pass through the point, one, one. Now
the midpoints of these chords form their own

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:20.330
locus called E2, E2 will be all the possible
midpoints, to get E2 notice that the chord

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:28.170
will be perpendicular to a line from the centre
to the midpoint. And so if we call our midpoint

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:36.300
(h, k) then the condition for E2 will be that
those two lines were perpendicular, their

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:42.130
gradients will multiply to be minus one, we
arrange that and you have the equation for

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:43.360
E2.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.400
You'd think that finding E1 and E2 would be
all the hard work done, because that was pretty

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:55.130
tricky. But then the exam gets downright nasty
by trying to trick you with some of the multi

00:10:55.130 --> 00:11:03.440
choice answers. They say the point four fifths
and seven fifths does not lie in E2. Now those

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:10.580
coordinates will satisfy the equation for
E2. So it looks like that point is in E2.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:16.840
But you're supposed to notice that that point
they gave you is actually the midpoint of

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:25.230
a chord that would have passed through P.
And since P isn't actually in E1, then neither

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:32.600
is the chord that would originate from it.
So B is an answer, because that point will

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.250
not be an E2.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:40.861
This was my working to figure that out. I
found the equation of a line through the point

00:11:40.861 --> 00:11:47.840
they gave you and (1,1), that was the equation
there. And then I found the equation of a

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:54.070
line that would pass through P and (1,1).
And it was the same equation.

00:11:54.070 --> 00:12:00.450
For C you can find that it is not an answer,
because this point does not satisfy the equation

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:07.180
of E2. And for D, you will find that it is
also an answer, because of this point does

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:11.070
not satisfy the equation of E1.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:17.960
So that's been a look at a question from each
paper. In this case, I did find the JEE question

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:24.060
harder, since the geometry was more difficult
to understand. But of course, you can't judge

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:30.000
a whole exam on one question. There will be
questions that are harder and easier than

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.350
these ones. So the battle is still on. In
fact, it was more difficult for me to comb

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:43.500
through the gaokao questions, since it's difficult
to get translations. So if you know of a particularly

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:49.670
tricky one, then send in a link to it. And
same with any JEE questions. I do enjoy taking

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:55.860
a look at which ones you found the hardest.
I've also seen comments from other countries,

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:01.650
including Turkey, Korea and Brazil, and I
hope to cover your exams in the future. It's

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:07.460
worth remembering here that the gaokao and
the JEE. are measuring different groups of

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:13.470
students for different reasons on different
topics. And the difficulty of questions within

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:20.000
an exam I find is often more determined by
the kind of preparation you've done, and perhaps

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:26.540
how similar the questions are to those of
previous years. Clearly, a lot of hard work

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:34.290
goes into preparing for either of these exams.
Thanks to my Patreon supporters for enabling

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:40.700
me to make these videos and a special shout
out to today's Patreon Cat of the Day Glacier.

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:44.899
Thanks for watching to the end of the video.
I hope to see you next time.

